      "text": "To solve the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of \\( y \\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nWe start by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5 to make the coefficients of \\( y \\) equal:\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22,\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\nNow we add these two equations together to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60) \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\nNow that we have \\( x = -2 \\), we substitute this value back into the first original equation to solve for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\nAdding 6 to both sides:\n\\[\n-5y = -5 \\implies y = 1.\n\\]\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we substitute \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\) back into both original equations:\n1. For the first equation:\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5(1) = -6 - 5 = -11,\n\\]\nwhich is true.\n2. For the second equation:\n\\[\n7(-2) + 2(1) = -14 + 2 = -12,\n\\]\nwhich is true.\nTherefore, the solution is correct. The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
