      "text": "To solve the given system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe will use the method of elimination. The goal is to eliminate one of the variables by making the coefficients of that variable equal in both equations.\n\nFirst, let's eliminate \\( y \\). To do this, we need to find a common multiple of the coefficients of \\( y \\) in both equations, which are \\(-5\\) and \\(2\\). The least common multiple of \\(5\\) and \\(2\\) is \\(10\\). Therefore, we will multiply the first equation by \\(2\\) and the second equation by \\(5\\) to make the coefficients of \\( y \\) equal in magnitude but opposite in sign.\n\nMultiplying the first equation by \\(2\\):\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the second equation by \\(5\\):\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\n\nNow we have the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n6x - 10y = -22, \\\\\n35x + 10y = -60.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nNext, we add these two equations together to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60) \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( x = -2 \\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\( y \\). Let's use the first equation:\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11 \\implies 3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11 \\implies -5y = -11 + 6 \\implies -5y = -5 \\implies y = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
