      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n3x - 5y = -11, \\\\\n7x + 2y = -12,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nwe will use the method of elimination. The goal is to eliminate one of the variables by combining the equations. First, we will eliminate \\( y \\).\n\nTo do this, we need to make the coefficients of \\( y \\) in both equations equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. We can achieve this by multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 5:\n\n\\[\n2(3x - 5y) = 2(-11) \\implies 6x - 10y = -22,\n\\]\n\\[\n5(7x + 2y) = 5(-12) \\implies 35x + 10y = -60.\n\\]\n\nNow, we add these two new equations together to eliminate \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n(6x - 10y) + (35x + 10y) = -22 + (-60).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side, the \\( y \\) terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n6x + 35x = -82 \\implies 41x = -82.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-82}{41} = -2.\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( x = -2 \\), we substitute this value back into one of the original equations to solve for \\( y \\). We will use the first equation:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\( x = -2 \\):\n\n\\[\n3(-2) - 5y = -11 \\implies -6 - 5y = -11.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n-6 - 5y = -11 \\implies -5y = -11 + 6 \\implies -5y = -5 \\implies y = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is the ordered pair:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, 1)}.\n\\]",
